00:04:16.279 --> 00:04:18.740
to deliver to their customers.

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:21.700
So a broad range
of things, and I'm

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:23.780
sure we'll get into
some of the discussions

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:25.660
on specific details.

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:28.140
One of the interesting
things here

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:34.680
is PwC-- we migrated away
from our legacy workplace

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:38.400
productivity tools over
to Google for Work.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:41.700
And now I'm leading a
number of initiatives

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:44.760
and developing
tools and solutions

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:46.370
on top of the Google
Cloud platform

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:47.980
that we're delivering
to our clients.

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:49.300
MODERATOR: Great, thank you.

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:51.240
John, can you talk
for Accenture?

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:53.790
JOHN CROSTHWAIT: I'm
glad to be here as well.

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:56.680
Most of you may have heard
of Accenture as well, much

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:58.080
like PwC.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:00.920
We're a large global
management consulting firm,

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:04.380
we have about 400,000
resources around the world.

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:07.380
And we're really
organized resource-wise

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:11.310
around two key dimensions--
one is capability.

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:15.044
All of our consultants
have a deep acumen

00:05:15.044 --> 00:05:16.710
in a particular
capability, whether it's

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:19.830
strategy, consulting,
operations, digital,

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:21.460
or technology.

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:25.249
And what we've found in
addressing large enterprise

00:05:25.249 --> 00:05:27.540
cloud transformation is that
each of these capabilities

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:30.800
play a very key role in a
successful transformation--

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:32.990
whether you're defining
the vision in the roadmap,

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:37.120
standing up a particular
cloud implementation,

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:41.360
or designing an op model on how
we organize resources ongoing.

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:43.630
The other dimension is industry.

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:48.310
And industry dictates where we
have individuals support which

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:49.180
clients.

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:51.030
And while most of
the considerations

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:54.080
we look at in considering
a cloud journey

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:56.710
are broadly applicable
across the market,

00:05:56.710 --> 00:05:58.870
we do find that there
is some industry

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:02.510
nuance that can affect any
individual company's cloud

00:06:02.510 --> 00:06:03.350
journey.

00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:05.280
So for example in
media, we might

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:07.910
find that we start in the
digital media workflow space,

00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:09.740
or with a large
telco we might start

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:13.900
with their omnichannel
platform and the like.

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:15.960
All of our customers
are consuming

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:20.130
cloud today, whether it's
individual, SaaS, products,

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:22.680
platforms like GCP
where they're building

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:26.180
custom applications and
the like, or buying storage

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:28.230
and compute.

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:31.540
And we've found that
about 80% of all companies

00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:35.490
are using infrastructure as a
service to some degree today,

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:37.390
and the result of this
pattern of consumption

00:06:37.390 --> 00:06:39.590
is really these
hybrid environments.

00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:42.660
And we've found about
50% of all companies

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:45.730
have hybrid cloud
strategies, and we

00:06:45.730 --> 00:06:47.530
think that is a
blueprint that is going

00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:49.580
to continue into the future.

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:51.870
And it's guided
how we've invested

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:55.180
in our cloud capability,
in serving the market.

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:56.480
A couple of examples, I guess.

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:00.810
We have an organization
called Cloud First, where

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:05.490
we house about 21,000
skilled resources

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:09.860
on a broad set of leading
cloud platforms and products.

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:14.837
And we've spent years and
considerable R&amp;D resources

00:07:14.837 --> 00:07:16.920
in developing something
called the Accenture Cloud

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:23.740
Platform, which is an open
multi-vendor platform that

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:28.316
enables organizations to
effectively and efficiently

00:07:28.316 --> 00:07:29.440
manage hybrid environments.

00:07:31.929 --> 00:07:33.470
So at any rate, I
hope that gives you

00:07:33.470 --> 00:07:36.749
a little bit of insight
in how we view the market.

00:07:36.749 --> 00:07:38.790
MIKE KAVIS: Mike Kavis
here, I'm glad to be here.

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:40.331
You may've not heard
of us, but we're

00:07:40.331 --> 00:07:43.650
Cloud Technology Partners based
out of Boston, Massachusetts.

00:07:43.650 --> 00:07:46.700
Our consultants live
everywhere-- I'm out of Tampa.

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:48.480
We're about a
100-person company.

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:50.860
We're the born-in-a-cloud
type company,

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:52.910
so a lot of our people--
including myself--

00:07:52.910 --> 00:07:54.710
come from the enterprise.

00:07:54.710 --> 00:07:56.740
Some people run data
centers, I myself

00:07:56.740 --> 00:07:58.910
have worked on distributed
systems and big data

00:07:58.910 --> 00:08:00.900
systems for years.

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:04.081
And our goal is to
accelerate the cloud,

00:08:04.081 --> 00:08:05.830
accelerate whether you
move into the cloud

00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:07.850
or build the cloud--
whatever you're doing,

00:08:07.850 --> 00:08:11.080
we have a lot of IP and
documented processes

00:08:11.080 --> 00:08:13.170
of how to get you there
as fast as possible.

00:08:13.170 --> 00:08:14.420
And we iterate through things.

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:16.020
So that's our story.

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:18.690
We do everything from cloud
migration, cloud strategy,

00:08:18.690 --> 00:08:21.190
to dev ops, to containers,
technologies, you name it.

00:08:21.190 --> 00:08:24.839
We cover the broad spectrum.

00:08:24.839 --> 00:08:26.630
UDAY AYYAGARI: Hi, my
name is Uday Ayyagari

00:08:26.630 --> 00:08:28.990
and I come from Tech Mahindra.

00:08:28.990 --> 00:08:32.720
And we are a global
SI for Google,

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:35.909
and we have a very
large practice

00:08:35.909 --> 00:08:38.630
in various different
areas of cloud.

00:08:38.630 --> 00:08:42.384
We have about 120,000
employees across the globe.

00:08:42.384 --> 00:08:44.550
You probably haven't heard
much about Tech Mahindra,

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:49.410
but we are predominantly all
over the globe-- APAC, ANZ,

00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:51.340
and the Americas.

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:57.210
And we are engaged actively
in a lot of telecom-- telecom

00:08:57.210 --> 00:09:00.360
being one of our
largest sectors.

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:03.160
And then we are heavy
into automobile industry,

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:04.450
several different verticals.

00:09:04.450 --> 00:09:07.870
We come with a strong practice
in advisory services, business

00:09:07.870 --> 00:09:09.400
transformation projects.

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:13.500
And we have over 50 different
services and product

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:14.990
frameworks.

00:09:14.990 --> 00:09:18.160
So the key aspect of our
go-to-market strategy

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:20.760
is to take solutions--
it's basically

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:25.660
a solution-based approach where
we have a number of frameworks

00:09:25.660 --> 00:09:28.800
there, including the cloud
migration strategy that we've

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:30.027
going to talk about.

00:09:30.027 --> 00:09:32.360
And also we have different
service offerings and product

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:35.430
offerings across the
various different industries

00:09:35.430 --> 00:09:37.250
that I talked
about-- predominantly

00:09:37.250 --> 00:09:39.770
in analytics and
telecommunications,

00:09:39.770 --> 00:09:41.970
telecommunications with
analytics and automobile.

00:09:44.770 --> 00:09:46.450
MODERATOR: Great, thanks.

00:09:46.450 --> 00:09:48.954
Why don't we do a
quick market survey?

00:09:48.954 --> 00:09:50.620
So can you talk a
little bit-- and maybe

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:53.860
we'll start with you, Uday--
on the trends you're seeing?

00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:55.999
What are customers trying
to do in the cloud?

00:09:55.999 --> 00:09:57.748
And where have they
seen the most success?

00:09:57.748 --> 00:10:00.410
UDAY AYYAGARI: Yeah, so
thanks for the question.

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:04.400
So we have noticed
a lot of heavy trend

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:11.210
towards migrating-- basically,
cloud is here to stay.

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:13.650
And there's a lot
of stress on moving

00:10:13.650 --> 00:10:16.160
their applications, mostly
the on-premise applications.

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:19.660
One of the forces may be
moving over from the CapEx

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:21.940
to an OpEx model.

00:10:21.940 --> 00:10:25.480
The focus is mostly on
getting onto the cloud.

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:28.770
And there's a lot of
confusion and fear

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:36.170
and lack of experience that's
driving a lot of this strategy

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:37.710
towards moving
over to the cloud.

00:10:37.710 --> 00:10:45.030
So we are noticing a lot
of movement, so to speak,

00:10:45.030 --> 00:10:48.130
like in moving some of the
reporting, the DR applications

00:10:48.130 --> 00:10:50.420
over to the cloud.

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:53.575
So that's the primary focus.

00:10:53.575 --> 00:10:55.700
MODERATOR: Are you seeing
anything different, Mike?

00:10:55.700 --> 00:10:57.750
MIKE KAVIS: Yeah, we're seeing
a lot of movement to the cloud,

00:10:57.750 --> 00:11:00.600
both from native development--
but one of the big drivers

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:02.090
is data center consolidation.

00:11:02.090 --> 00:11:03.766
So a lot of companies
have 30 and they

00:11:03.766 --> 00:11:07.927
need to get to five by x date,
and that's driving the lift

00:11:07.927 --> 00:11:08.760
and shift mentality.

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:11.320
We don't have time to
change the architecture,

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:13.440
we just need to get
there, consolidate,

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:16.127
get our expenses down, and
then the next generation

00:11:16.127 --> 00:11:17.710
is, how do I modernize
and really take

00:11:17.710 --> 00:11:21.803
advantage of the high-level
features of the cloud?

00:11:21.803 --> 00:11:22.386
MODERATOR: OK.

00:11:22.386 --> 00:11:24.040
John, anything to add?

00:11:24.040 --> 00:11:25.340
JOHN CROSTHWAIT: Yeah.

00:11:25.340 --> 00:11:28.120
One thing I'd share, I guess,
is that each year Accenture

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:31.200
publishes a technology vision.

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:34.650
We do this to identify what are
the trends that we think are

00:11:34.650 --> 00:11:37.040
going to affect our clients
in a significant way,

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:40.170
in an 18-to-36-month
planning horizon?

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:42.530
And one of the themes in
this year's tech vision

00:11:42.530 --> 00:11:45.650
was around the platform economy.

00:11:45.650 --> 00:11:47.890
And part of this exercise
includes a survey--

00:11:47.890 --> 00:11:51.250
we surveyed about 3,500
technology executives

00:11:51.250 --> 00:11:51.970
around the world.

00:11:51.970 --> 00:11:54.900
And it was
interesting-- about 85%

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:57.000
came back with the
statement that they believe

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:00.490
that their company is going to
move away from selling products

00:12:00.490 --> 00:12:03.030
today to selling business
outcomes through managed

00:12:03.030 --> 00:12:05.370
services in the next
three to five years.

00:12:05.370 --> 00:12:07.120
And we think this is
a trend that's really

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:09.710
going to push traditional
companies to get

00:12:09.710 --> 00:12:11.610
into the platform
business, as sort

00:12:11.610 --> 00:12:14.860
of their foundational method
of delivering products

00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:15.906
and services.

00:12:15.906 --> 00:12:17.780
And you can kind of
start to see some of that

00:12:17.780 --> 00:12:19.230
show up in the market today.

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:22.200
You've got companies
like General Motors

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:26.280
who's implementing My Chevrolet,
which is a platform that

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:28.740
integrates the buyers of
their electric vehicle--

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:35.210
The Bolt-- to upload car
vehicle performance information,

00:12:35.210 --> 00:12:37.410
trend it, benchmark it
against other drivers,

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:39.720
send it to product
development and the like.

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:41.590
Or companies like
Kaiser Permanente,

00:12:41.590 --> 00:12:45.020
who's establishing a platform
to collect and manage

00:12:45.020 --> 00:12:47.500
electronic medical
data, but also

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:50.030
integrate all the
broad ecosystem

00:12:50.030 --> 00:12:52.410
players that help
them collectively

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:54.930
serve their patients today.

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:58.490
Or even General Electric, who's
starting to pivot-- move away

00:12:58.490 --> 00:13:01.670
from their traditional
industrial manufacturing roots

00:13:01.670 --> 00:13:04.350
and getting more into
software and data.

00:13:04.350 --> 00:13:06.380
So we think this
trend will continue.

00:13:06.380 --> 00:13:10.270
And as these epicenters in
industries start to mature,

00:13:10.270 --> 00:13:12.700
they're going to have to
support millions of consumers,

00:13:12.700 --> 00:13:15.110
thousands of ecosystem
partners, operate

00:13:15.110 --> 00:13:18.060
in an always-on
environment, which

00:13:18.060 --> 00:13:22.790
is going to require them to work
with web-scale large-platform--

00:13:22.790 --> 00:13:25.665
trusted platform
providers like Google

00:13:25.665 --> 00:13:26.790
and the like going forward.

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:29.280
And I think Google
happens to be one

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:33.550
that is well-positioned to serve
this market in a lot of ways.

00:13:33.550 --> 00:13:35.700
We believe in their
proven ability

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:38.700
to look at their
core product and data

00:13:38.700 --> 00:13:41.360
and create new, interesting,
value-added services-- much

00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:46.210
like the Vision API we saw
earlier in the keynote.

00:13:46.210 --> 00:13:50.930
MIKE PEARL: Yeah, I would just
add similarly, at PwC each year

00:13:50.930 --> 00:13:56.980
we survey over 1,400-- this
year it was over 1,400 CEOs

00:13:56.980 --> 00:13:58.030
globally.

00:13:58.030 --> 00:14:00.280
And it's a pretty
broad split of sizes

00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:05.010
of organizations, a global
mix across all industries.

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:08.210
And for the fourth
year in a row,

00:14:08.210 --> 00:14:13.160
the number one item
top-of-mind for CEOs

00:14:13.160 --> 00:14:16.560
was the impact
technology is going

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:18.280
to have on their business.

00:14:18.280 --> 00:14:21.190
And both from the
perspective of how

00:14:21.190 --> 00:14:24.390
are they going to
leverage new technologies

00:14:24.390 --> 00:14:26.710
to create a
competitive advantage,

00:14:26.710 --> 00:14:34.160
but also a concern about smaller
organizations that have access

00:14:34.160 --> 00:14:36.740
to platforms like the
Google Cloud platform

00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:42.770
that can quickly take IP and
create solutions and really

00:14:42.770 --> 00:14:47.790
disrupt what our clients are
trying to do in the market.

00:14:47.790 --> 00:14:50.640
And so a lot of
the trend that we

00:14:50.640 --> 00:14:53.870
see from that-- and actually,
interestingly enough this year

00:14:53.870 --> 00:14:55.060
there was a bit of a pivot.

00:14:55.060 --> 00:14:57.090
It's not just about
the technology,

00:14:57.090 --> 00:15:02.830
but how is that technology being
leveraged to drive innovation,

00:15:02.830 --> 00:15:07.800
and innovation with
respect to product,

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:11.400
with respect to how they're
operating their business?

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:13.930
And this unique
kind of interplay

00:15:13.930 --> 00:15:17.300
between how technology gets
leveraged for innovation

00:15:17.300 --> 00:15:22.180
and how you're going to
leverage and identify talent

00:15:22.180 --> 00:15:23.620
to allow you to do that.

00:15:23.620 --> 00:15:30.200
And it's really all with respect
to moving faster in an ever

00:15:30.200 --> 00:15:32.270
more dynamic marketplace.

00:15:32.270 --> 00:15:37.430
And so for instance,
we-- working with Google

00:15:37.430 --> 00:15:40.130
and on the Google Cloud
platform-- one of the things

00:15:40.130 --> 00:15:44.640
that we thought was very
important was to-- the number

00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:47.550
one item that comes
up is around security

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:49.670
is to really take
that head-on, where

00:15:49.670 --> 00:15:52.790
we've developed our security
analytics platform leveraging

00:15:52.790 --> 00:15:55.350
the Google Cloud
platform with this notion

00:15:55.350 --> 00:16:01.300
that security of exclusion
is really a bit of a myth.

00:16:01.300 --> 00:16:04.770
You're always compromised
at some level.

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:06.980
And so the goal is,
how quickly can you

00:16:06.980 --> 00:16:10.160
identify what's happening
within your environment,

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:12.980
pinpoint where it is,
and remediate that?

00:16:12.980 --> 00:16:16.240
And so that's just an
example, similar to what

00:16:16.240 --> 00:16:20.280
we're seeing with in our
survey at our clients.

00:16:20.280 --> 00:16:22.280
But really starting to
look at, how can you

00:16:22.280 --> 00:16:24.730
leverage this
technology to create

00:16:24.730 --> 00:16:28.492
something that is differentiated
for you in the market?

00:16:28.492 --> 00:16:30.950
MODERATOR: You know I appreciate
you, Mike, for bringing up

00:16:30.950 --> 00:16:32.587
security.

00:16:32.587 --> 00:16:34.170
I think one of the
themes that we just

00:16:34.170 --> 00:16:36.420
heard is sort of this
opportunity that's

00:16:36.420 --> 00:16:38.030
afforded by technology
and by cloud,

00:16:38.030 --> 00:16:40.660
but also the business
transformation that's

00:16:40.660 --> 00:16:43.080
happening, and the uncertainty,
and some of the fear that

00:16:43.080 --> 00:16:44.110
revolves around that.

00:16:44.110 --> 00:16:46.774
So can you talk a little
bit to some of the hurdles

00:16:46.774 --> 00:16:48.190
that you're seeing
companies face,

00:16:48.190 --> 00:16:49.754
and what you guys
are doing to help

00:16:49.754 --> 00:16:50.920
them overcome those hurdles?

00:16:50.920 --> 00:16:53.300
MIKE PEARL: Yeah,
well when you think

00:16:53.300 --> 00:16:56.500
about conservative
organizations,

00:16:56.500 --> 00:16:58.310
our brand is relatively
conservative.

00:16:58.310 --> 00:17:02.600
So we spent-- and I was
up close and personal

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:05.800
when we made our decision to
move over to the Google Cloud

00:17:05.800 --> 00:17:08.520
platform and Google for Work.

00:17:08.520 --> 00:17:12.730
There was a lot of
questions, understandably,

00:17:12.730 --> 00:17:15.550
because we're in a regulated
environment, our audit

00:17:15.550 --> 00:17:16.170
business.

00:17:16.170 --> 00:17:18.599
And a lot of questions
around security

00:17:18.599 --> 00:17:20.910
and how we would manage that.

00:17:20.910 --> 00:17:23.910
And working with Google,
we were able to get

00:17:23.910 --> 00:17:26.204
through the security questions.

00:17:26.204 --> 00:17:27.829
But the biggest thing
that we're seeing

00:17:27.829 --> 00:17:31.440
is, quite frankly,
regulation compliance.

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:35.200
And regulation understandably
isn't keeping pace

00:17:35.200 --> 00:17:38.520
with the rate of
change in technology.

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:44.730
And so oftentimes where there
is amazing technology that

00:17:44.730 --> 00:17:47.350
allows organizations
to connect better,

00:17:47.350 --> 00:17:50.630
move faster in the market,
there are regulations

00:17:50.630 --> 00:17:52.820
that were written
many years ago that

00:17:52.820 --> 00:17:55.580
didn't contemplate the
technologies that are there

00:17:55.580 --> 00:17:56.110
today.

00:17:56.110 --> 00:18:00.220
And so a lot of what we
spend time with our clients

00:18:00.220 --> 00:18:02.520
helping them work
with is understanding

00:18:02.520 --> 00:18:04.910
what those regulations
are, understanding

00:18:04.910 --> 00:18:08.610
how they can apply
those regulations--

00:18:08.610 --> 00:18:10.410
in the context of
some of the hurdles

00:18:10.410 --> 00:18:14.380
that we would see-- apply those
regulations in understanding

00:18:14.380 --> 00:18:16.811
how they can still get
benefit from the technology.

00:18:16.811 --> 00:18:17.561
MODERATOR: Got it.

00:18:17.561 --> 00:18:18.060
Great.

00:18:18.060 --> 00:18:19.970
John, are you seeing
anything different?

00:18:19.970 --> 00:18:22.250
JOHN CROSTHWAIT: You know,
you mentioned security.

00:18:22.250 --> 00:18:23.625
The only thing
I'd add to that is

00:18:23.625 --> 00:18:27.830
that definitely with all
the cyber breaches recently,

00:18:27.830 --> 00:18:30.660
that's obviously created
a lot of anxiety.

00:18:30.660 --> 00:18:34.120
One pattern we have
seen is that as clients

00:18:34.120 --> 00:18:36.980
get closer to the providers
that they've selected,

00:18:36.980 --> 00:18:40.140
like a Google, they
inevitably push through it.

00:18:40.140 --> 00:18:43.020
And I think it's more about just
engaging with the right vendors

00:18:43.020 --> 00:18:44.080
that you trust.

00:18:44.080 --> 00:18:48.470
And at the end of the day,
public cloud consumption

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:52.360
is rising at 44% a year right
now, and on premise at 9%.

00:18:52.360 --> 00:18:56.540
So the move to
public cloud is not

00:18:56.540 --> 00:18:59.160
stopping because of security,
but it's obviously always

00:18:59.160 --> 00:19:00.400
the first thing.

00:19:00.400 --> 00:19:03.130
I think the other
common barrier that we

00:19:03.130 --> 00:19:05.700
find is just the mental
barrier of where to start.

00:19:05.700 --> 00:19:07.606
It can seem very daunting.

00:19:07.606 --> 00:19:09.980
There's a lot of-- do we do
a traditional lift and shift?

00:19:09.980 --> 00:19:11.370
Do we new on new?

00:19:11.370 --> 00:19:14.840
What is the right approach?

00:19:14.840 --> 00:19:18.960
And honestly, the guidance
that we always give

00:19:18.960 --> 00:19:23.580
is to really take the time to
put together a cloud strategy

00:19:23.580 --> 00:19:26.740
and a vision and a roadmap, and
all the due diligence that that

00:19:26.740 --> 00:19:30.080
implies, to allow you
to move with confidence.

00:19:30.080 --> 00:19:33.460
Because it really is a
broad transformation.

00:19:33.460 --> 00:19:38.230
It's not about procurement
or finding a new hardware

00:19:38.230 --> 00:19:39.300
infrastructure provider.

00:19:39.300 --> 00:19:43.250
It's a broad transformation
that needs to be

00:19:43.250 --> 00:19:45.180
given that level of diligence.

00:19:45.180 --> 00:19:48.380
And then I think, lastly,
another hurdle we've seen

00:19:48.380 --> 00:19:50.160
is just internal talent.

00:19:50.160 --> 00:19:52.340
So as you might guess,
most organizations

00:19:52.340 --> 00:19:55.930
don't have a breadth of talent
sitting there that understand

00:19:55.930 --> 00:19:59.750
how to design cloud based
architectures in high velocity

00:19:59.750 --> 00:20:01.250
environments and the like.

00:20:01.250 --> 00:20:05.510
And so as you create your
vision and your roadmap

00:20:05.510 --> 00:20:08.640
to roll out cloud capability
within your organization,

00:20:08.640 --> 00:20:13.030
having a talent plan that can
keep pace with that roadmap

00:20:13.030 --> 00:20:15.410
is crucial.

00:20:15.410 --> 00:20:16.290
MODERATOR: Great.

00:20:16.290 --> 00:20:16.797
Mike?

00:20:16.797 --> 00:20:18.380
MIKE KAVIS: The other
thing is there's

00:20:18.380 --> 00:20:23.340
a lot of mythology about cloud
security, stuff like that.

00:20:23.340 --> 00:20:25.370
So when of the first
things we do is we come in.

00:20:25.370 --> 00:20:27.036
And the first thing
we do is a workshop.

00:20:27.036 --> 00:20:29.760
And we kind of go through
all that, talk about myth

00:20:29.760 --> 00:20:31.060
versus reality.

00:20:31.060 --> 00:20:33.730
And we have a lot
of case studies

00:20:33.730 --> 00:20:36.250
of our own clients in financial
institutions and health

00:20:36.250 --> 00:20:39.630
institutions that this is how
they overcome those hurdles.

00:20:39.630 --> 00:20:42.450
And we have one customer who
was in the security space

00:20:42.450 --> 00:20:43.754
when Heartbleed was going on.

00:20:43.754 --> 00:20:45.670
Well, how long does it
take you to patch that?

00:20:45.670 --> 00:20:48.430
Well, it takes months
to provision a server.

00:20:48.430 --> 00:20:49.680
So I kind of drew this chart.

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:51.096
And I said, OK,
over three months,

00:20:51.096 --> 00:20:53.410
this is how many Heartbleed
type things happen.

00:20:53.410 --> 00:20:56.239
So by the time you get rid of
this one, there's four more.

00:20:56.239 --> 00:20:58.780
There's another reason to take
advantage of the cloud, right?

00:20:58.780 --> 00:20:59.696
Infrastructures, code.

00:20:59.696 --> 00:21:04.070
You can actually turn servers or
VMs around in minutes or days.

00:21:04.070 --> 00:21:06.929
So it's kind of leveling
the playing field.

00:21:06.929 --> 00:21:07.970
There's a lot of chatter.

00:21:07.970 --> 00:21:09.490
And a lot of it
comes from companies

00:21:09.490 --> 00:21:12.057
that are not benefiting
from the public cloud.

00:21:12.057 --> 00:21:13.890
Hardware companies,
private cloud companies.

00:21:13.890 --> 00:21:15.431
So it's a leveling
the playing field,

00:21:15.431 --> 00:21:19.340
showing real use cases
of real success stories.

00:21:19.340 --> 00:21:22.430
Just kind of
leveling the playing.

00:21:22.430 --> 00:21:26.060
UDAY AYYAGARI: You know,
so from our perspective,

00:21:26.060 --> 00:21:28.940
when we go to a
client-- typically,

00:21:28.940 --> 00:21:31.570
nine out of 10 cases, it's
basically the security.

00:21:31.570 --> 00:21:33.170
Security is job
number one always.

00:21:33.170 --> 00:21:35.810
And we've talked
enough about that.

00:21:35.810 --> 00:21:38.271
However, we need to--
part of the methodology

00:21:38.271 --> 00:21:40.270
and the framework that
we offer, on a side note,

00:21:40.270 --> 00:21:45.790
is we have a complete,
fully mature framework where

00:21:45.790 --> 00:21:47.860
we have-- we went to the
client and understand

00:21:47.860 --> 00:21:50.250
what this security
architecture is currently,

00:21:50.250 --> 00:21:52.527
what the security assets
are, and move them over

00:21:52.527 --> 00:21:53.360
to the new platform.

00:21:53.360 --> 00:21:57.290
But it's not as simple as like
just doing the lift and shift.

00:21:57.290 --> 00:21:58.570
So security one side.

00:21:58.570 --> 00:22:01.620
Now we have-- that needs
to be, basically, addressed

00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:02.760
with performance.

00:22:02.760 --> 00:22:05.770
Security performance, and
then we have loss of control.

00:22:05.770 --> 00:22:09.580
So there's a perception
among the customer community

00:22:09.580 --> 00:22:11.570
about losing all
this infrastructure.

00:22:11.570 --> 00:22:13.742
They've invested
already heavily into it.

00:22:13.742 --> 00:22:15.200
What are they going
to do about it?

00:22:15.200 --> 00:22:16.590
So sometimes it's
a balancing act.

00:22:16.590 --> 00:22:18.006
It's not a
straightforward answer.

00:22:18.006 --> 00:22:20.100
It's a one-size-fits-all
does not work out here.

00:22:20.100 --> 00:22:22.740
They need to weigh their
options on is it, like,

00:22:22.740 --> 00:22:23.570
do they move now?

00:22:23.570 --> 00:22:25.869
Is it the right time to
move over to cloud and adopt

00:22:25.869 --> 00:22:26.910
the best cloud practices?

00:22:26.910 --> 00:22:29.270
Because it's an imperative.

00:22:29.270 --> 00:22:31.990
I mean, it's not a joke
question of choice.

00:22:31.990 --> 00:22:34.720
As against, do they
want to still hold

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:37.779
on to their infrastructure and
the investments that they have.

00:22:37.779 --> 00:22:39.820
So there's a loss of
control I was talking about.

00:22:39.820 --> 00:22:41.820
Now the other aspect,
which is slightly tougher,

00:22:41.820 --> 00:22:44.770
is what we face is
the business aspect.

00:22:44.770 --> 00:22:46.100
So we have changed management.

00:22:46.100 --> 00:22:48.830
There's this whole perception
about loss of jobs.

00:22:48.830 --> 00:22:51.690
I mean, what traditionally used
to be the hard jobs that we

00:22:51.690 --> 00:22:55.700
talked about in a moment back,
they could be transformed.

00:22:55.700 --> 00:22:58.360
So how do we retrain,
reskill workers?

00:22:58.360 --> 00:23:00.410
How does the management
look at that?

00:23:00.410 --> 00:23:02.210
So there's various
different aspects to it.

00:23:02.210 --> 00:23:06.950
Now part of that also goes
along with the legality

00:23:06.950 --> 00:23:09.420
of like the data
residency issues.

00:23:09.420 --> 00:23:13.310
Are they really ready for
moving over to that next,

00:23:13.310 --> 00:23:17.504
take that big leap, to probably
segregate the data and moves

00:23:17.504 --> 00:23:19.670
part out of it over to the
cloud, which is what-- so

00:23:19.670 --> 00:23:21.071
all these decisions.

00:23:21.071 --> 00:23:22.570
We need to have a
holistic approach,

00:23:22.570 --> 00:23:24.278
and that's what we
provide as a framework

00:23:24.278 --> 00:23:27.340
through a heavily
[INAUDIBLE] approach.

00:23:27.340 --> 00:23:30.350
Business application,
portfolio analysis that we do.

00:23:30.350 --> 00:23:33.180
And then we come up with
very strong recommendations,

00:23:33.180 --> 00:23:35.245
which eventually is up
to the client to adopts.

00:23:35.245 --> 00:23:36.650
I mean, guided recommendation.

00:23:36.650 --> 00:23:42.370
So in all this, like I
think Mike was stating,

00:23:42.370 --> 00:23:44.057
the speed is breakneck.

00:23:44.057 --> 00:23:45.890
We're not talking about
a four-year platform

00:23:45.890 --> 00:23:47.640
or a two-year like
on a road map.

00:23:47.640 --> 00:23:49.460
It's probably three months.

00:23:49.460 --> 00:23:52.890
And you have to be very
realistic, because there's

00:23:52.890 --> 00:23:56.360
so many options out there, so
many points where you can--

00:23:56.360 --> 00:23:58.270
points of failure, so to speak.

00:23:58.270 --> 00:24:01.250
So through this
entire journey, it

00:24:01.250 --> 00:24:03.840
becomes very imperative
for our customers.

00:24:03.840 --> 00:24:05.340
And that's what
they turn back to us

00:24:05.340 --> 00:24:09.750
for is they need to have a
trusted adviser by their side.

00:24:09.750 --> 00:24:12.240
And that's exactly
what we provide.

00:24:12.240 --> 00:24:14.840
So we had, just as a
case in point, just

00:24:14.840 --> 00:24:18.440
for the audience also
to know, we have about,

00:24:18.440 --> 00:24:20.980
just taking the figures
recently with the last quarter,

00:24:20.980 --> 00:24:26.220
with 26,095
successful application

00:24:26.220 --> 00:24:28.210
migrations that we've
done over to the cloud.

00:24:28.210 --> 00:24:30.670
And this is across
193 companies.

00:24:30.670 --> 00:24:33.680
[INAUDIBLE], ANZ, and we're
talking the entire globe.

00:24:33.680 --> 00:24:37.210
So we come with deep
expertise, deep experience.

00:24:37.210 --> 00:24:41.820
We pretty much have a
majority of the questions,

00:24:41.820 --> 00:24:45.090
even though the migration
methodology and the framework

00:24:45.090 --> 00:24:47.081
itself is evolving.

00:24:47.081 --> 00:24:48.330
They're very strong use cases.

00:24:48.330 --> 00:24:50.660
And you can stop by
the booth if you want

00:24:50.660 --> 00:24:51.994
some more details about it.

00:24:51.994 --> 00:24:53.660
MIKE KAVIS: And can
I harp on one point?

00:24:53.660 --> 00:24:54.070
MODERATOR: Please.

00:24:54.070 --> 00:24:55.944
MIKE KAVIS: He mentioned
that another barrier

00:24:55.944 --> 00:24:57.510
is that fear of lost jobs.

00:24:57.510 --> 00:25:00.540
And what we really see is
when you go to the cloud,

00:25:00.540 --> 00:25:03.270
you automate all the stuff,
you don't lose the job.

00:25:03.270 --> 00:25:05.830
You let these people work
on higher-value tasks.

00:25:05.830 --> 00:25:07.680
And that's something
they need to be told.

00:25:07.680 --> 00:25:09.129
Because a lot of
the stuff they do

00:25:09.129 --> 00:25:11.420
they wish they had time to
do these higher-level tasks.

00:25:11.420 --> 00:25:13.211
And that's one of the
beauties of the cloud

00:25:13.211 --> 00:25:15.830
is it takes away that mundane
stuff that you have to work on.

00:25:15.830 --> 00:25:17.320
It frees you up
to work on things

00:25:17.320 --> 00:25:18.700
that really move the needle.

00:25:18.700 --> 00:25:20.574
So no one's job
getting replaced.

00:25:20.574 --> 00:25:22.490
You're going to work on
more important things.

00:25:22.490 --> 00:25:22.820
UDAY AYYAGARI: Exactly.

00:25:22.820 --> 00:25:24.445
It's [INAUDIBLE]
versus context, right?

00:25:24.445 --> 00:25:25.710
MODERATOR: Yeah.

00:25:25.710 --> 00:25:28.545
So kind of to the end of
the business transformation

00:25:28.545 --> 00:25:30.170
and the organizational
transformation--

00:25:30.170 --> 00:25:31.864
to your point-- that
cloud's enabling.

00:25:31.864 --> 00:25:33.280
There's going to
be customers that

00:25:33.280 --> 00:25:37.074
are kind of on different ends
of the risk tolerance spectrum.

00:25:37.074 --> 00:25:38.490
I think, John, you
were mentioning

00:25:38.490 --> 00:25:41.340
sort of that choice between
lift and shift or new on new.

00:25:41.340 --> 00:25:44.001
So what types of
things, John, are you

00:25:44.001 --> 00:25:46.250
advocating to some of your
more conservative customers

00:25:46.250 --> 00:25:52.080
who maybe don't want to jump
in head first from day one.

00:25:52.080 --> 00:25:53.830
JOHN CROSTHWAIT: So
we still recommend

00:25:53.830 --> 00:25:56.480
that they do think through
and have a deliberate

00:25:56.480 --> 00:25:58.780
strategy and a road
map, even if that road

00:25:58.780 --> 00:26:02.510
map is more conservative and
aligns more with their culture.

00:26:02.510 --> 00:26:06.240
But a lot of places
they will start

00:26:06.240 --> 00:26:09.050
is new on new is the easiest.

00:26:09.050 --> 00:26:11.010
Where it's green field.

00:26:11.010 --> 00:26:14.540
They're likely
consuming cloud today

00:26:14.540 --> 00:26:20.890
in their interactive businesses,
whether it's mobile or online.

00:26:20.890 --> 00:26:23.530
And then any sort of
major inflection point

00:26:23.530 --> 00:26:25.610
in which they're
doing re-architecture

00:26:25.610 --> 00:26:29.960
is an opportunity that we
encourage them to embrace.

00:26:29.960 --> 00:26:32.670
I think the other
area, too, is SaaS,

00:26:32.670 --> 00:26:34.240
which, when we
think of cloud, you

00:26:34.240 --> 00:26:36.090
think of custom
development and the like.

00:26:36.090 --> 00:26:42.120
But moving from instead of
doing high-velocity development

00:26:42.120 --> 00:26:44.230
in the public cloud
with sensitive data,

00:26:44.230 --> 00:26:46.880
which is where everyone
gets a lot of anxiety,

00:26:46.880 --> 00:26:50.200
adopting SaaS platforms
as well as a way

00:26:50.200 --> 00:26:52.360
to ease down that road.

00:26:52.360 --> 00:26:54.710
And then the second is
we definitely encourage

00:26:54.710 --> 00:26:57.050
them to adopt a platform.

00:26:57.050 --> 00:27:00.100
When you at-- if you're going
to move at a very, I'd say,

00:27:00.100 --> 00:27:03.710
more deliberate
or measured pace,

00:27:03.710 --> 00:27:05.580
it's hard to bring
the organization along

00:27:05.580 --> 00:27:07.270
if you just say, hey,
we're doing cloud.

00:27:07.270 --> 00:27:07.770
Right?

00:27:07.770 --> 00:27:11.010
And so, you know, here's some
broad based tools and whatnot.

00:27:11.010 --> 00:27:14.750
But if you come in with
a deliberate platform

00:27:14.750 --> 00:27:17.932
and also bundle it more
with-- not just, hey,

00:27:17.932 --> 00:27:19.640
we're doing cloud,
but you're bundling it

00:27:19.640 --> 00:27:22.070
with a different way of
delivering it, right?

00:27:22.070 --> 00:27:24.205
And so it probably is
integrated with a number

00:27:24.205 --> 00:27:26.080
of other initiatives
that you have in flight,

00:27:26.080 --> 00:27:27.840
whether that's your
agile transformation,

00:27:27.840 --> 00:27:29.770
or maybe you're
implementing DevOps.

00:27:29.770 --> 00:27:31.950
And bundling that
together to where

00:27:31.950 --> 00:27:33.810
you're bringing your
organization along

00:27:33.810 --> 00:27:36.720
and this whole notion of,
yes, we do things differently,

00:27:36.720 --> 00:27:39.980
and we're producing
different architectures,

00:27:39.980 --> 00:27:42.130
and we're moving at
a different pace.

00:27:42.130 --> 00:27:46.290
And if that is underpinned by
a very deliberate platform,

00:27:46.290 --> 00:27:47.830
it's much easier
for an organization

00:27:47.830 --> 00:27:50.360
to get their arms around.

00:27:50.360 --> 00:27:52.560
MODERATOR: So, Mike, John
just talked about new

00:27:52.560 --> 00:27:54.500
on new being maybe
the lowest-risk thing,

00:27:54.500 --> 00:27:57.770
but also having more of an
organization-wide strategy

00:27:57.770 --> 00:27:59.420
for cloud adoption and road map.

00:27:59.420 --> 00:28:01.910
What are you guys doing
that's maybe different,

00:28:01.910 --> 00:28:02.990
or in addition to that?

00:28:02.990 --> 00:28:04.800
MIKE PEARL: Well, I
mean, I would agree.

00:28:08.780 --> 00:28:12.410
The question is
not if, but when.

00:28:12.410 --> 00:28:15.780
There's too much benefit
in moving and adopting

00:28:15.780 --> 00:28:19.570
cloud for organizations not
to move in that direction.

00:28:19.570 --> 00:28:24.060
And so, as John pointed out, we
do the same with our clients.

00:28:24.060 --> 00:28:27.150
Be thoughtful about
what you're ready for,

00:28:27.150 --> 00:28:29.260
as well as what
you're not ready for.

00:28:29.260 --> 00:28:33.390
And we also encourage
people to invest the time

00:28:33.390 --> 00:28:38.360
to really understand what is
the business value that you're

00:28:38.360 --> 00:28:39.400
going to get out of it.

00:28:39.400 --> 00:28:41.470
Because, as you said,
if you say we're

00:28:41.470 --> 00:28:47.490
going to do cloud for cloud
sake, that loses people.

00:28:47.490 --> 00:28:48.530
They want to understand.

00:28:48.530 --> 00:28:55.300
If you can articulate the speed
at which teams can collaborate

00:28:55.300 --> 00:28:59.600
differently across the globe
and compare current state

00:28:59.600 --> 00:29:03.900
to future state
leveraging cloud platform,

00:29:03.900 --> 00:29:10.090
or articulate how transformation
or development of technology

00:29:10.090 --> 00:29:14.090
assets can go from a
procurement exercise

00:29:14.090 --> 00:29:20.860
into months of development
to weeks and outside a month.

00:29:20.860 --> 00:29:23.070
Those are the types of
things where people then

00:29:23.070 --> 00:29:26.480
start to understand, OK,
here's the value that I'm

00:29:26.480 --> 00:29:27.760
going to get out of that.

00:29:27.760 --> 00:29:31.770
And then you're having
a risk discussion

00:29:31.770 --> 00:29:33.370
on very different terms.

00:29:33.370 --> 00:29:36.330
You're agreed that
you want to move

00:29:36.330 --> 00:29:38.740
in the direction
of business value,

00:29:38.740 --> 00:29:40.897
but you're thoughtful
about the risks

00:29:40.897 --> 00:29:43.230
that you're willing to take
in moving in that direction.

00:29:43.230 --> 00:29:43.944
MODERATOR: Great.

00:29:43.944 --> 00:29:44.860
Mike, anything to add?

00:29:44.860 --> 00:29:46.443
MIKE KAVIS: One of
the-- there's a lot

00:29:46.443 --> 00:29:48.330
of areas to cover
on transformation

00:29:48.330 --> 00:29:51.270
and organizational change--
one area that gets forgotten

00:29:51.270 --> 00:29:53.280
a lot about-- a lot of
times it's developer-led,

00:29:53.280 --> 00:29:56.070
so it's get the first app and
forget about the operations

00:29:56.070 --> 00:29:56.570
side.

00:29:56.570 --> 00:29:57.970
How do I run this?

00:29:57.970 --> 00:30:00.030
How do I keep track of
costs and everything?

00:30:00.030 --> 00:30:03.420
And in the data center, whether
your processes are good or not,

00:30:03.420 --> 00:30:06.110
you know them, and they've
been in place for 20 years.

00:30:06.110 --> 00:30:08.550
Now you're going to this new
platform, you don't have any.

00:30:08.550 --> 00:30:09.050
Right?

00:30:09.050 --> 00:30:12.350
So in the transformation,
we make sure, in sequence,

00:30:12.350 --> 00:30:14.370
while you're building
architectures,

00:30:14.370 --> 00:30:17.790
you're also building
out how do you run?

00:30:17.790 --> 00:30:18.550
Look at the stuff.

00:30:18.550 --> 00:30:20.340
So that's a big piece
that gets missed.

00:30:20.340 --> 00:30:23.220
It's really easy to get
the first few in the cloud.

00:30:23.220 --> 00:30:24.590
Then you need to get 1,000.

00:30:24.590 --> 00:30:26.600
If you don't have
how do I run it,

00:30:26.600 --> 00:30:29.640
it becomes worse than
what you had before.

00:30:29.640 --> 00:30:33.060
So you've got to think
about the whole spectrum.

00:30:33.060 --> 00:30:35.140
UDAY AYYAGARI: You
know, so the trend

00:30:35.140 --> 00:30:39.310
that we've seen mostly-- so
can you repeat the question?

00:30:39.310 --> 00:30:41.080
What are the hurdles?

00:30:41.080 --> 00:30:43.720
MODERATOR: The question
is, for the more

00:30:43.720 --> 00:30:47.130
conservative
companies, how are you

00:30:47.130 --> 00:30:48.909
helping these companies
kind of adjust

00:30:48.909 --> 00:30:50.200
to that clouded option journey?

00:30:50.200 --> 00:30:54.290
We heard a little bit about the
roadmap, planning, the process

00:30:54.290 --> 00:30:55.720
management, and so on.

00:30:55.720 --> 00:30:56.930
Do you have anything to add?

00:30:56.930 --> 00:30:58.840
UDAY AYYAGARI:
Mostly, like I said

00:30:58.840 --> 00:31:03.040
earlier, there's fear and the
experience that's lacking.

00:31:03.040 --> 00:31:04.840
So they don't know what
they're up against.

00:31:04.840 --> 00:31:10.420
Even though there is starting
at their advisory board level,

00:31:10.420 --> 00:31:12.950
at the board level, there is a
mandate to move to the cloud.

00:31:12.950 --> 00:31:14.790
But they don't know exactly how.

00:31:14.790 --> 00:31:19.610
So our approach, generally
we advocate an approach

00:31:19.610 --> 00:31:22.870
where we just want to
move bit-sized steps first

00:31:22.870 --> 00:31:24.320
before we make the big leaps.

00:31:24.320 --> 00:31:27.090
So definitely, there's
no one-size-fits-all,

00:31:27.090 --> 00:31:30.410
but typically we would go
through this entire portfolio

00:31:30.410 --> 00:31:33.090
analysis where we do this
entire marketing of different

00:31:33.090 --> 00:31:35.170
applications portfolio
segmentation.

00:31:35.170 --> 00:31:37.620
Their recommendations
typically come across

00:31:37.620 --> 00:31:41.190
in terms of what
other stuff that is

00:31:41.190 --> 00:31:43.130
qualified for lift and shift.

00:31:43.130 --> 00:31:44.440
And then lift and shift is one.

00:31:44.440 --> 00:31:45.750
Re-architecting
and re-engineering

00:31:45.750 --> 00:31:48.291
is another one where there may
be small tweaks here and there

00:31:48.291 --> 00:31:49.360
that may be required.

00:31:49.360 --> 00:31:52.530
However, that probably requires
some more in-depth recoding

00:31:52.530 --> 00:31:54.686
kind of stuff, which is
a slightly expensive.

00:31:54.686 --> 00:31:57.060
And there are applications
which you don't want to touch.

00:31:57.060 --> 00:31:58.526
Just it's better off on premise.

00:31:58.526 --> 00:32:00.650
And you probably may want
to think about sunsetting

00:32:00.650 --> 00:32:01.950
those are applications as well.

00:32:01.950 --> 00:32:05.630
So these are-- so just a
very interesting example--

00:32:05.630 --> 00:32:10.220
we were working with a media
company not so long ago.

00:32:10.220 --> 00:32:12.880
This has been there
since like the 1960s.

00:32:12.880 --> 00:32:15.940
So there are a whole bunch of
code and COBOL-driven programs

00:32:15.940 --> 00:32:17.360
which are just lying out there.

00:32:17.360 --> 00:32:20.790
And when we actually engaged
with the entire cross-section

00:32:20.790 --> 00:32:23.880
of the IT community,
half of them

00:32:23.880 --> 00:32:28.560
did not even know what
those applications did.

00:32:28.560 --> 00:32:31.320
So this something that-- well,
a typical answer was this

00:32:31.320 --> 00:32:32.514
was there since I joined.

00:32:32.514 --> 00:32:33.930
I don't know exactly
what it does.

00:32:33.930 --> 00:32:36.388
And there's a fear of uprooting
that apparition because you

00:32:36.388 --> 00:32:39.450
don't know what-- why
is there to be unplug it

00:32:39.450 --> 00:32:40.570
by just moving it?

00:32:40.570 --> 00:32:42.520
So there are some
eye-opening [INAUDIBLE]

00:32:42.520 --> 00:32:45.114
which come as a part of
this entire exercise.

00:32:45.114 --> 00:32:46.780
But the recommendation,
again, typically

00:32:46.780 --> 00:32:50.462
comes-- there are
non-revenue-facing

00:32:50.462 --> 00:32:51.920
applications,
non-business-critical

00:32:51.920 --> 00:32:55.400
applications, that you want
to kind of put it on the side.

00:32:55.400 --> 00:32:56.400
Just have a plan for it.

00:32:56.400 --> 00:32:58.610
Put it in the roadmap
probably nine months later.

00:32:58.610 --> 00:33:01.510
But you want to just address
the low-hanging fruit right now.

00:33:01.510 --> 00:33:03.080
And what are those
low-hanging fruit?

00:33:03.080 --> 00:33:04.830
Is there a typical
reporting applications?

00:33:04.830 --> 00:33:07.150
DR applications--
disaster recovery--

00:33:07.150 --> 00:33:09.440
which don't have a high
rate of [INAUDIBLE] kind

00:33:09.440 --> 00:33:11.230
of requirements, even if it is.

00:33:11.230 --> 00:33:15.887
So the idea is to have
adopters and evangelists

00:33:15.887 --> 00:33:17.470
internally from
within the company who

00:33:17.470 --> 00:33:19.690
can advocate for you.

00:33:19.690 --> 00:33:22.730
Just a few successes here
and there and slowly--

00:33:22.730 --> 00:33:26.239
and we've actually seen this
translate into mega projects,

00:33:26.239 --> 00:33:28.530
starting with just moving a
few reporting applications.

00:33:28.530 --> 00:33:29.840
So you'd be surprised.

00:33:29.840 --> 00:33:31.520
Now one more point
that I'd like to add

00:33:31.520 --> 00:33:36.270
here is-- the kind of
adoption that you'd

00:33:36.270 --> 00:33:41.820
see within these
enterprises is exponential.

00:33:41.820 --> 00:33:43.340
The graph is not
linear like this.

00:33:43.340 --> 00:33:45.700
It just goes like--
it's a straight go.

00:33:45.700 --> 00:33:48.220
When you're talking about
five, 10, 20 applications,

00:33:48.220 --> 00:33:49.500
reporting applications.

00:33:49.500 --> 00:33:53.360
After that they're just asking
you-- the conversation changes.

00:33:53.360 --> 00:33:55.919
They're talking about moving
the [INAUDIBLE] applications,

00:33:55.919 --> 00:33:57.960
[INAUDIBLE] applications,
auto management systems

00:33:57.960 --> 00:34:00.400
which are directly
attached to the revenue.

00:34:00.400 --> 00:34:03.152
And then the security
conversation starts,

00:34:03.152 --> 00:34:05.610
because that's where the entire
conversation starts, right?

00:34:05.610 --> 00:34:09.989
So the thing is, while we're
moving over to the cloud,

00:34:09.989 --> 00:34:11.810
how can we change
our security posture?

00:34:11.810 --> 00:34:13.969
It's not about how can I
protect my existing data

00:34:13.969 --> 00:34:16.350
and move my existing
artifacts over to the cloud.

00:34:16.350 --> 00:34:17.570
That is not the conversation.

00:34:17.570 --> 00:34:20.840
It's mostly about how can
it change, because they know

00:34:20.840 --> 00:34:22.670
the security person
that they have hired

00:34:22.670 --> 00:34:25.350
is probably not the
best, and they probably

00:34:25.350 --> 00:34:27.909
are two steps behind the
hackers who is trying

00:34:27.909 --> 00:34:30.230
to break into the applications.

00:34:30.230 --> 00:34:34.730
But coming with cloud, we kind
of make the point gradually.

00:34:34.730 --> 00:34:37.385
Your applications are
safer sitting in the cloud.

00:34:37.385 --> 00:34:39.010
And that becomes the
easy conversation.

00:34:39.010 --> 00:34:40.969
So the whole thing
starts with wait

00:34:40.969 --> 00:34:42.969
for the low-hanging
fruits, and then

00:34:42.969 --> 00:34:47.050
making the conversation for,
like a few steps first, crawl

00:34:47.050 --> 00:34:48.350
before you run.

00:34:48.350 --> 00:34:50.190
MODERATOR: So it sounds
like the goal here

00:34:50.190 --> 00:34:51.929
with these more
conservative enterprises

00:34:51.929 --> 00:34:56.650
is to sort of generate internal
support for cloud adoption

00:34:56.650 --> 00:34:58.420
through small,
low-risk wins, and try

00:34:58.420 --> 00:35:01.820
to use that to grow that
engagement internally.

00:35:01.820 --> 00:35:04.110
Now, we got a chance
to talk earlier

00:35:04.110 --> 00:35:06.149
to Spotify and to Disney
who've, obviously,

00:35:06.149 --> 00:35:07.940
been much more aggressive,
more progressive

00:35:07.940 --> 00:35:09.160
with their cloud adoption.

00:35:09.160 --> 00:35:10.854
How does that change
your ability--

00:35:10.854 --> 00:35:12.520
what kind of dynamic
things are you guys

00:35:12.520 --> 00:35:15.117
doing with those companies
that have fewer constraints

00:35:15.117 --> 00:35:17.200
and are willing to kind
of jump into that business

00:35:17.200 --> 00:35:18.880
transformational a
little more quickly?

00:35:18.880 --> 00:35:19.380
Mike.

00:35:19.380 --> 00:35:25.460
MIKE KAVIS: Yeah, well, some of
the things that we highlighted

00:35:25.460 --> 00:35:32.450
here, the sky's the limit, if
you will, on what you can do.

00:35:32.450 --> 00:35:35.990
Organizations that are
moving very aggressively

00:35:35.990 --> 00:35:39.340
towards the cloud
and leveraging it

00:35:39.340 --> 00:35:45.420
is-- again, the value that
they're able to generate,

00:35:45.420 --> 00:35:50.630
and the speed at which they're
able to-- with our clients,

00:35:50.630 --> 00:35:53.720
we focus on this concept
around high-velocity IT.

00:35:53.720 --> 00:35:57.670
And it's based on cloud
infrastructure development

00:35:57.670 --> 00:36:03.470
platforms that allow for the
leveraging a DevOps model,

00:36:03.470 --> 00:36:05.980
that once you have the
platform in place--

00:36:05.980 --> 00:36:08.650
and you highlighted from
some of your research--

00:36:08.650 --> 00:36:13.680
this platform economy
that is coming up,

00:36:13.680 --> 00:36:16.250
companies that start to move
in that direction really

00:36:16.250 --> 00:36:21.180
start to realize benefits
maybe that they didn't even

00:36:21.180 --> 00:36:21.860
think about.

00:36:21.860 --> 00:36:24.440
You start to put
things into the cloud,

00:36:24.440 --> 00:36:27.550
develop services,
develop solutions.

00:36:27.550 --> 00:36:30.550
And as you continue to
move in that direction,

00:36:30.550 --> 00:36:34.530
you learn more about what
you can be doing differently.

00:36:34.530 --> 00:36:39.960
And that gets back to some of
our research on our CEO survey

00:36:39.960 --> 00:36:45.100
where this whole notion of
providing the proper tools

00:36:45.100 --> 00:36:49.390
to allow people to
innovate internally really

00:36:49.390 --> 00:36:52.180
does change the game
for organizations

00:36:52.180 --> 00:36:54.782
that are ready to adopt
it in an aggressive way.

00:36:54.782 --> 00:36:55.490
MODERATOR: Right.

00:36:55.490 --> 00:36:57.640
So John, Mike was
talking a little bit

00:36:57.640 --> 00:36:59.280
about developer velocity.

00:36:59.280 --> 00:37:02.720
We also heard a lot about things
like machine learning and data

00:37:02.720 --> 00:37:03.820
capabilities.

00:37:03.820 --> 00:37:07.450
What's sort of moving the needle
for your most sophisticated

00:37:07.450 --> 00:37:09.350
customers?

00:37:09.350 --> 00:37:11.430
JOHN CROSTHWAIT: So, let's see.

00:37:11.430 --> 00:37:14.220
I think a lot of it
is about-- and some

00:37:14.220 --> 00:37:16.470
of these kind of build on
a point that Mike made,

00:37:16.470 --> 00:37:20.572
which is the promise
of cloud-- companies

00:37:20.572 --> 00:37:22.030
go into it for a
number of reasons.

00:37:22.030 --> 00:37:23.750
They feel like they have to.

00:37:23.750 --> 00:37:26.370
They're looking for efficiency.

00:37:26.370 --> 00:37:27.990
They want velocity and the like.

00:37:27.990 --> 00:37:29.900
But the reality is
that it's really

00:37:29.900 --> 00:37:33.510
one element of a broader
transformation in changing

00:37:33.510 --> 00:37:34.950
the way they
deliver technologies

00:37:34.950 --> 00:37:36.140
for their business.

00:37:36.140 --> 00:37:39.342
And often it's driven by, maybe,
a couple of different factors.

00:37:39.342 --> 00:37:41.800
One is that they need to keep
pace with the business, which

00:37:41.800 --> 00:37:45.130
is now operating in a market
that demands a quicker

00:37:45.130 --> 00:37:49.560
time to market, more rapid
and iterative innovation.

00:37:49.560 --> 00:37:53.930
And at the same time, maybe
you've come to a maturity level

00:37:53.930 --> 00:37:57.319
on some traditional models
of offshoring and outsourcing

00:37:57.319 --> 00:37:58.860
and the like, and
now you need to get

00:37:58.860 --> 00:38:01.318
that next level of productivity
out of technology delivery.

00:38:01.318 --> 00:38:04.820
And so looking at a number
of different concepts

00:38:04.820 --> 00:38:09.550
in high velocity, or new
IT as Mike mentioned,

00:38:09.550 --> 00:38:13.000
really come
together-- really need

00:38:13.000 --> 00:38:14.920
to be implemented in
aggregate to achieve

00:38:14.920 --> 00:38:16.150
the promise of [INAUDIBLE].

00:38:16.150 --> 00:38:19.910
And that includes platforms
in cloud, but also

00:38:19.910 --> 00:38:25.610
DevOps and instrumentation
automation, and of course agile

00:38:25.610 --> 00:38:27.360
to get into iterative
development.

00:38:27.360 --> 00:38:29.310
And those three
together, particularly

00:38:29.310 --> 00:38:32.440
if you're moving fast, the
only way to sustain that change

00:38:32.440 --> 00:38:36.120
and not run into a
bunch of barriers

00:38:36.120 --> 00:38:38.140
after your initial
sprint out of the gate,

00:38:38.140 --> 00:38:40.540
is to ensure that you've
got the right op model.

00:38:40.540 --> 00:38:43.940
And so you're constructing
your organization

00:38:43.940 --> 00:38:46.450
to move with these
new tools and methods.

00:38:46.450 --> 00:38:49.300
And for developers, it's
a completely different way

00:38:49.300 --> 00:38:50.190
to operate.

00:38:50.190 --> 00:38:54.410
So for engineers, you've got
to double down on the culture

00:38:54.410 --> 00:38:59.950
and on the talent models such
that you can keep that pace.

00:38:59.950 --> 00:39:02.310
But that's what we
found is that if there

00:39:02.310 --> 00:39:04.970
is a lot of excitement, the
company has a strategy-- you

00:39:04.970 --> 00:39:07.437
know, I think Mike's
points around making sure

00:39:07.437 --> 00:39:09.520
that that vision ties to
goals that are measurable

00:39:09.520 --> 00:39:11.990
and everyone feels
accountable to,

00:39:11.990 --> 00:39:14.530
and then having this
comprehensive approach on how

00:39:14.530 --> 00:39:17.844
to get there would be my input.

00:39:17.844 --> 00:39:18.720
MODERATOR: Great.

00:39:18.720 --> 00:39:20.540
MIKE KAVIS: So I wrote
this post on Forbes

00:39:20.540 --> 00:39:23.310
a while back called "The Four
Stages of Cloud Competency."

00:39:23.310 --> 00:39:26.870
And it matches with the old
psychology four competencies.

00:39:26.870 --> 00:39:28.924
And at the first phase,
it's like you don't know,

00:39:28.924 --> 00:39:30.090
so therefore you don't like.

00:39:30.090 --> 00:39:32.630
So you don't know anything
about cloud, and it's bad.

00:39:32.630 --> 00:39:35.130
Then the second stage is,
I kind of know about it.

00:39:35.130 --> 00:39:37.880
So I go into it, and I
treat it like a data center.

00:39:37.880 --> 00:39:40.010
And the third stage is,
OK, I've got competencies,

00:39:40.010 --> 00:39:41.050
I've used the
cloud, and then you

00:39:41.050 --> 00:39:42.591
start really taking
advantage of some

00:39:42.591 --> 00:39:44.190
of these higher-level services.

00:39:44.190 --> 00:39:46.767
And the fourth level is,
I'm totally competent.

00:39:46.767 --> 00:39:49.350
And those are the companies that
are seeing the cloud as a way

00:39:49.350 --> 00:39:50.470
to transform the business.

00:39:50.470 --> 00:39:53.053
And those are like the Spotify
and those types of company that

00:39:53.053 --> 00:39:56.110
say, I can use this in
ways and change the way we

00:39:56.110 --> 00:39:58.070
price things that could
change business models.

00:39:58.070 --> 00:40:00.950
I can offer services I've
never offered before.

00:40:00.950 --> 00:40:03.294
And we've seen all those
types of customers.

00:40:03.294 --> 00:40:04.960
And there's a lot of
different examples.

00:40:04.960 --> 00:40:06.736
But really a lot
of how much-- like,

00:40:06.736 --> 00:40:08.110
there's a lot of
companies coming

00:40:08.110 --> 00:40:09.350
to the public cloud
for the first time,

00:40:09.350 --> 00:40:11.040
but they've been building
private clouds for two,

00:40:11.040 --> 00:40:11.540
three years.

00:40:11.540 --> 00:40:12.797
So they get cloud.

00:40:12.797 --> 00:40:14.630
And a lot of them are
coming to public cloud

00:40:14.630 --> 00:40:16.505
because they said, that's
a lot of hard work.

00:40:16.505 --> 00:40:18.540
I don't want to do a lot
of that work anymore.

00:40:18.540 --> 00:40:20.145
So you get that mix.

00:40:20.145 --> 00:40:22.020
The more experience
people have in the cloud,

00:40:22.020 --> 00:40:23.770
the more they move
up that stack.

00:40:23.770 --> 00:40:26.945
They start using these
quality services we saw today.

00:40:26.945 --> 00:40:28.570
But a lot of the
early people are like,

00:40:28.570 --> 00:40:30.028
I'm afraid of
lock-in, so they just

00:40:30.028 --> 00:40:31.360
treat it like a data center.

00:40:31.360 --> 00:40:33.510
And all they're really doing
is they have a virtual data

00:40:33.510 --> 00:40:33.860
center.

00:40:33.860 --> 00:40:34.950
They're still doing
all the same work

00:40:34.950 --> 00:40:36.140
they did in their
data center, they're

00:40:36.140 --> 00:40:37.265
just doing it on the cloud.

00:40:37.265 --> 00:40:39.946
They're missing a
big opportunity.

00:40:39.946 --> 00:40:41.320
MODERATOR: So,
Uday, any examples

00:40:41.320 --> 00:40:43.778
you can share to Mike's point
of companies that have really

00:40:43.778 --> 00:40:45.770
kind of gone to that next
level of abstraction,

00:40:45.770 --> 00:40:48.170
really using the
higher order services,

00:40:48.170 --> 00:40:51.630
and try to take full
advantage of the cloud?

00:40:51.630 --> 00:40:53.200
UDAY AYYAGARI: Yeah, absolutely.

00:40:53.200 --> 00:41:00.855
So most of my discussion
points would go around--

00:41:00.855 --> 00:41:01.980
[INAUDIBLE] versus context.

00:41:01.980 --> 00:41:03.810
We already know
about that, right?

00:41:03.810 --> 00:41:08.700
So like points that
John raised about,

00:41:08.700 --> 00:41:10.890
or he mentioned
about, DevOps, right?

00:41:10.890 --> 00:41:12.730
So what we're talking
about the velocity

00:41:12.730 --> 00:41:13.820
is phenomenally increased.

00:41:13.820 --> 00:41:14.850
We all know that.

00:41:14.850 --> 00:41:18.530
Now I'll give you
an example again.

00:41:18.530 --> 00:41:24.140
We're just doing a proof
of concept for a prospect.

00:41:24.140 --> 00:41:27.690
The core business model
centers around heavy compute,

00:41:27.690 --> 00:41:29.330
heavy computing, right?

00:41:29.330 --> 00:41:31.560
Now I don't want
to-- probably it's

00:41:31.560 --> 00:41:33.930
fair to differentiate also
the other clouds, which is

00:41:33.930 --> 00:41:35.910
what GCP brought to the table.

00:41:35.910 --> 00:41:40.460
Is what used take-- the
complex algorithms that

00:41:40.460 --> 00:41:43.057
would be fed into with
petabytes of data,

00:41:43.057 --> 00:41:45.640
that it would just dig into and
then come back with the result

00:41:45.640 --> 00:41:50.190
would take 45 minutes to an
hour for an average workload.

00:41:50.190 --> 00:41:52.510
Now we just do a
POC where we just

00:41:52.510 --> 00:41:54.680
got the same amount of
data and richer quality

00:41:54.680 --> 00:41:57.920
with richer analytics
with under 10 seconds.

00:41:57.920 --> 00:41:59.330
So this is just one case point.

00:41:59.330 --> 00:42:01.700
So just think of the
magnitude of savings.

00:42:01.700 --> 00:42:04.440
So we're talking 45
versus 10 seconds.

00:42:04.440 --> 00:42:07.440
Now, that's not the
whole value proposition.

00:42:07.440 --> 00:42:10.250
So, certainly, the moment we
just do this, the good news

00:42:10.250 --> 00:42:12.460
goes across like wildfire.

00:42:12.460 --> 00:42:19.435
Now they are not-- the
investors and senior management

00:42:19.435 --> 00:42:21.570
are essentially
looking at how they

00:42:21.570 --> 00:42:23.820
can scale this across
a vast portfolio

00:42:23.820 --> 00:42:25.170
for their application.

00:42:25.170 --> 00:42:28.720
So we're talking of significant,
enormous amounts of savings

00:42:28.720 --> 00:42:31.000
for operation efficiency.

00:42:31.000 --> 00:42:33.610
Now the Ph.D. folks
who are primarily

00:42:33.610 --> 00:42:35.870
working on the core
engineering and coming up

00:42:35.870 --> 00:42:38.166
with newer formulas,
so to speak,

00:42:38.166 --> 00:42:40.290
without giving out too much
of their business model

00:42:40.290 --> 00:42:41.940
and what they are exactly doing.

00:42:41.940 --> 00:42:44.481
They are sitting side-by-side
with the folks who are actually

00:42:44.481 --> 00:42:45.330
quoting it for them.

00:42:45.330 --> 00:42:47.060
And the result
are instantaneous.

00:42:47.060 --> 00:42:50.095
So we see the needle
moving heavily

00:42:50.095 --> 00:42:53.600
in terms of operation
efficiency, it's for real.

00:42:53.600 --> 00:42:55.280
So that's one example.

00:42:55.280 --> 00:43:01.830
And then we have so many
different efficiency services,

00:43:01.830 --> 00:43:03.260
[INAUDIBLE] being one of them.

00:43:03.260 --> 00:43:04.910
And then we have the Vision API.

00:43:04.910 --> 00:43:08.100
It just basically opens
more possibilities.

00:43:08.100 --> 00:43:11.420
And then, certainly, we have
a lot of visualization aspect

00:43:11.420 --> 00:43:13.332
to this entire
conversation that comes up.

00:43:13.332 --> 00:43:15.040
If you're talking
logistics, for example,

00:43:15.040 --> 00:43:17.390
we have a product for supply
chain visibility platform,

00:43:17.390 --> 00:43:21.130
which basically brings all the
entire supply chain staplers

00:43:21.130 --> 00:43:22.640
onto a common platform.

00:43:22.640 --> 00:43:24.490
It's basically, we
track inventory.

00:43:24.490 --> 00:43:30.250
Now on top of that, we're able
to bring in a very rich user

00:43:30.250 --> 00:43:33.250
look and feel, a great high
level of user experience

00:43:33.250 --> 00:43:34.590
in various different aspects.

00:43:34.590 --> 00:43:36.990
So the conversation
has certainly

00:43:36.990 --> 00:43:38.985
risen terms of the
capabilities that we

00:43:38.985 --> 00:43:41.670
can bring, how we can enable
the business using more

00:43:41.670 --> 00:43:43.160
enabling technologies.

00:43:43.160 --> 00:43:46.080
So there are
possibilities all around.

00:43:46.080 --> 00:43:49.860
So a lot of announcements that
we heard today in the morning,

00:43:49.860 --> 00:43:52.240
they directly address how
each of these services

00:43:52.240 --> 00:43:54.380
are greatly addressing
the business community.

00:43:54.380 --> 00:43:58.810
So it's hard to say
there's one single area,

00:43:58.810 --> 00:44:00.840
but then if you were
to speak about it,

00:44:00.840 --> 00:44:03.360
it's probably the time
to market capability.

00:44:03.360 --> 00:44:06.130
You have an idea,
bring it to the market,

00:44:06.130 --> 00:44:08.375
it's a matter of
probably-- I wouldn't

00:44:08.375 --> 00:44:13.000
say days or months or hours--
but it's drastically reduced.

00:44:13.000 --> 00:44:14.640
So you have time
to market and then

00:44:14.640 --> 00:44:16.760
operational efficiency are the
two things that come to my mind

00:44:16.760 --> 00:44:17.260
right now.

00:44:17.260 --> 00:44:18.822
But there are definitely more.

00:44:18.822 --> 00:44:19.530
MODERATOR: Great.

00:44:19.530 --> 00:44:20.154
Thanks.

00:44:20.154 --> 00:44:21.820
Now we're starting
to run short on time,

00:44:21.820 --> 00:44:24.030
so I appreciate all
your comments so far.

00:44:24.030 --> 00:44:25.690
If you can give the
audience maybe just

00:44:25.690 --> 00:44:29.070
one last parting thought on if
they're starting their cloud

00:44:29.070 --> 00:44:31.910
migration journey, what's the
one piece of advice you give.

00:44:31.910 --> 00:44:32.910
Starting with you, Uday.

00:44:32.910 --> 00:44:33.743
UDAY AYYAGARI: Sure.

00:44:36.100 --> 00:44:39.140
So one-size-fits-all
does not work.

00:44:39.140 --> 00:44:41.500
Cloud migration has to happen.

00:44:41.500 --> 00:44:42.070
OK?

00:44:42.070 --> 00:44:46.610
So we've read enough articles,
enough material out there,

00:44:46.610 --> 00:44:50.000
it's basically you want to focus
on your business versus you

00:44:50.000 --> 00:44:52.972
want to be maintaining a
whole bunch of metal boxes

00:44:52.972 --> 00:44:54.930
and then doing the
infrastructure kind of work,

00:44:54.930 --> 00:44:56.420
right?

00:44:56.420 --> 00:44:57.720
And still be left behind.

00:44:57.720 --> 00:45:02.900
Now the transition
will be pretty rapid.

00:45:02.900 --> 00:45:04.760
I think if you just
look five years back

00:45:04.760 --> 00:45:06.910
where we were compared
to the conversation we're

00:45:06.910 --> 00:45:10.860
having right now, it's
significantly different.

00:45:10.860 --> 00:45:14.170
What I think is, along
the way-- I mean,

00:45:14.170 --> 00:45:17.537
there are too many points of
failure, like I mentioned.

00:45:17.537 --> 00:45:19.120
Purely because of
the way your systems

00:45:19.120 --> 00:45:21.036
have been architected
or the kind of ecosystem

00:45:21.036 --> 00:45:23.750
that you survive in
or live in-- probably

00:45:23.750 --> 00:45:26.861
25 or 30 years old application
that's still surviving out

00:45:26.861 --> 00:45:27.360
there.

00:45:27.360 --> 00:45:29.060
It's time to do an overhaul.

00:45:29.060 --> 00:45:33.530
So you need a trusted
adviser during this journey.

00:45:33.530 --> 00:45:38.060
With us having done over 26,000
applications migrations, put

00:45:38.060 --> 00:45:41.340
across all our
client base, we come

00:45:41.340 --> 00:45:44.690
with a very rich methodology
and a framework to kind

00:45:44.690 --> 00:45:47.280
of hand-hold you to success.

00:45:47.280 --> 00:45:48.196
MODERATOR: Great.

00:45:48.196 --> 00:45:50.170
MIKE KAVIS: Real quick,
because we're at zero.

00:45:50.170 --> 00:45:51.700
Start with a proven methodology.

00:45:51.700 --> 00:45:53.420
Start small.

00:45:53.420 --> 00:45:55.792
Iterate, learn,
iterate, iterate.

00:45:55.792 --> 00:45:56.500
MODERATOR: Great.

00:45:56.500 --> 00:45:57.570
That's good.

00:45:57.570 --> 00:45:58.945
JOHN CROSTHWAIT:
Quickly, then, I

00:45:58.945 --> 00:46:00.650
would say, operate with a plan.

00:46:00.650 --> 00:46:03.950
Create a plan that's integrated
with your broader technology

00:46:03.950 --> 00:46:06.010
delivery transformation.

00:46:06.010 --> 00:46:09.390
Don't forget model
talent and culture.

00:46:09.390 --> 00:46:12.840
And the last is treat it like
the level of transformation

00:46:12.840 --> 00:46:15.590
that it is and get
senior sponsorship that's

00:46:15.590 --> 00:46:18.440
going to be visible
throughout the journey

00:46:18.440 --> 00:46:20.750
to bring everybody along.

00:46:20.750 --> 00:46:22.820
MIKE PEARL: Yeah, I
would quickly, say

00:46:22.820 --> 00:46:25.610
touching on a point
that I raised earlier,

00:46:25.610 --> 00:46:30.050
is this notion of it
is a transformation.

00:46:30.050 --> 00:46:33.480
You want to bring your
organization with you.

00:46:33.480 --> 00:46:36.310
So identify the
business outcomes

00:46:36.310 --> 00:46:38.930
that you're looking
for and how cloud's

00:46:38.930 --> 00:46:45.320
going to help you achieve that,
and that will drive a greater

00:46:45.320 --> 00:46:46.800
willingness for adoption.

00:46:46.800 --> 00:46:49.740
And I would agree with
John on this notion

00:46:49.740 --> 00:46:53.550
of being thoughtful and diligent
about setting that plan.

00:46:53.550 --> 00:46:56.347
Once you establish
your business goals,

00:46:56.347 --> 00:46:58.180
be thoughtful about how
you're going to move

00:46:58.180 --> 00:46:59.622
towards achieving those.

00:46:59.622 --> 00:47:00.330
MODERATOR: Great.

00:47:00.330 --> 00:47:01.909
Well, thank you all very much.

00:47:01.909 --> 00:47:02.450
Please help--

